{"cuisine/syrian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/syrian-cuisine","n":"Syrian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Syria","intro":[["Syrian cuisine is an Arab and Mediterranean cuisine that traces back to ancient civilization in Syria and the region. Syrian specialties makes use of eggplant, zucchini, garlic, meat (mostly from lamb and sheep), sesame seeds, rice, chickpeas, fava beans, lentils, steak, cabbage, cauliflower, vine leaves, pickled turnips, cucumbers, tomatoes, olive oil, lemon juice, mint, pistachios, honey and fruits."],["Selections of appetizers known as mezze are customarily served along with Arabic bread before the Syrian meal's main course, which is followed by coffee, with sweet confections or fruits at will. Many recipes date from at least the 13th century."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"syrian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Syria: Recipes from Home","ref":"cookbook/syria-recipes-from-home","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Itab Azzam and Dina Mousawi, 2017","note":"Home recipes gathered from Syrian women in exile; ISBN is the US edition titled Our Syria.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780762490523","suggested":true},{"name":"Sumac: Recipes and Stories from Syria","ref":"cookbook/sumac-recipes-and-stories-from-syria","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Anas Atassi","note":"Homs-born author's family recipes and memories of Syria.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781623718978","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":7,"links":[["Eggplant jam","dish/eggplant-jam"],["Halawet el jibn","dish/halawet-el-jibn"],["Hummus","dish/hummus"],["Jibneh Arabieh","dish/jibneh-arabieh"],["Kibbeh safarjaliyeh","dish/kibbeh-safarjaliyeh"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"],["Taboon bread","dish/taboon-bread"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Syrup","ingredient/syrup"],["Akkawi cheese","ingredient/akkawi-cheese"],["Pistachio","ingredient/pistachio"],["Qishta","ingredient/qishta"],["Rose water","ingredient/rose-water"],["Chickpea","ingredient/chickpea"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Tahini","ingredient/tahini"],["Goat milk","ingredient/goat-milk"],["Sheep milk","ingredient/sheep-milk"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Kibbeh","ingredient/kibbeh"],["Pomegranate","ingredient/pomegranate"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Baking powder","ingredient/baking-powder"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Syrian cooking","count":1,"links":[["Syrian Jewish cuisine","cuisine/syrian-jewish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/cuisine-of-albania":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-albania","n":"Albanian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Albania","intro":[["Albanian cuisine is a representative of the cuisine of the Mediterranean. It is also an example of the Mediterranean diet based on the importance of olive oil, fruits, vegetables, and fish. The cooking traditions of the Albanian people are diverse in consequence of the environmental factors that are more importantly suitable for the cultivation of nearly every kind of herbs, vegetables, and fruits. Olive oil is the most ancient and commonly used vegetable fat in Albanian cooking, produced since antiquity throughout the country particularly along the coasts."],["Hospitality is a fundamental custom of Albanian society and serving food is integral to the hosting of guests and visitors. It is not infrequent for visitors to be invited to eat and drink with locals. The medieval Albanian code of honor, called besa, resolved to look after guests and strangers as an act of recognition and gratitude."],["Albanian cuisine can be divided into three major regional cuisines. The cuisine of the northern region has a rural, coastal and mountainous origin. Meat, fish and vegetables are central to the cuisine of the northern region. The people there use many kinds of ingredients that usually grow in the region, including potatoes, carrots, maize, beans, and cabbage, and also cherries, walnuts and almonds. Garlic and onions are as well important components to the local cuisine and added to almost every dish."],["The cuisine of the central region is threefold of rural, mountainous and coastal. The central region is the flattest and rich in vegetation and biodiversity as well as culinary specialties. It has Mediterranean characteristics due to its proximity to the sea, which is rich in fish. Dishes here include several meat specialties and desserts of all kinds."],["In the south, the cuisine is composed of two components:  the rural products of the field including dairy products, citrus fruits and olive oil, and coastal products, i.e. seafood. Those regions are particularly conducive to raising animals, as pastures and feed resources are abundant."],["Besides garlic, onions are arguably the country's most widely used ingredient. Albania is ranked fifth in the world in terms of onion consumption per capita."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-albania"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Albania"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"My Albanian Food","ref":"site/myalbanianfood-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"myalbanianfood.com","note":"Albanian home recipes shared by an Albanian cook.","url":"https://www.myalbanianfood.com/?p=936","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Albanian Table (Sofra Shqiptare)","ref":"cookbook/the-albanian-table-sofra-shqiptare","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Merita Dovolani","note":"Bilingual collection of traditional Albanian family recipes.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":4,"links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["İmam bayıldı","dish/imam-bay-ld"],["Kabuni","dish/kabuni"],["Tavë kosi","dish/tave-kosi"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Aubergine","ingredient/aubergine"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Clov","ingredient/clov"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Soured milk","ingredient/soured-milk"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yogurt","ingredient/yogurt"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Albanian cooking","count":1,"links":[["Kosovan cuisine","cuisine/kosovan-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"ingredient/parsley":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/parsley","n":"Parsley","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 17 dishes across 17 cuisines","intro":[["Parsley, or garden parsley (Petroselinum crispum), is a species of flowering plant in the family Apiaceae that is native to Greece, the Balkans, Algeria and Morocco.  It has been introduced and naturalized in Europe and elsewhere in the world with suitable climates, and is widely cultivated as a herb and a vegetable."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions"