"ingredient/egg"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Salt cod","ingredient/salt-cod"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Bacalhau","ingredient/bacalhau"],["Olive","ingredient/olive"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Mentha","ingredient/mentha"],["Carrots","ingredient/carrots"]]}]},"ingredient/chorizo":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/chorizo","n":"Chorizo","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 6 cuisines","intro":[["Chorizo is a type of pork sausage originating from the Iberian Peninsula. It is made in many national and regional varieties in several countries on different continents. Some of these varieties are quite different from each other, occasionally leading to confusion or disagreements over the names and identities of the products in question."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-chorizo"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chorizo"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":6,"groups":[["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",[["Asado","dish/asado"]]],["Bolivian cuisine","cuisine/bolivian-cuisine",[["Locro","dish/locro"]]],["Ecuadorian cuisine","cuisine/ecuadorian-cuisine",[["Locro","dish/locro"]]],["Galician cuisine","cuisine/galician-cuisine",[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"]]],["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine",[["Locro","dish/locro"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Blood sausage","ingredient/blood-sausage"],["Llama","ingredient/llama"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Lacón","ingredient/lacon"],["Phaseolus vulgaris","ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris"],["Pig fat","ingredient/pig-fat"],["Rapini","ingredient/rapini"],["White bean","ingredient/white-bean"],["Beef jerky","ingredient/beef-jerky"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Hot sauce","ingredient/hot-sauce"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"]]}]},"ingredient/kashkaval":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/kashkaval","n":"Kashkaval","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Kashkaval is a type of cheese made from the milk of cows, sheep, goats, or a mixture thereof, popular in the Balkans, Turkey, and the Levant. In Hungary, kashkaval refers specifically to this type of cheese, whereas, in the rest of the Balkans, Turkey and the Levant, the term is often used to refer to all yellow cheeses (or even any cheese other than sirene). In English-language menus in Bulgaria, kashkaval is translated as 'yellow cheese' (whereas sirene is usually translated as 'white cheese' or simply 'cheese')."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-kashkaval"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kashkaval"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Sephardic Jewish cuisine","cuisine/sephardic-jewish-cuisine",[["Bulemas","dish/bulemas"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Feta cheese","ingredient/feta-cheese"]]}]},"ingredient/ajika":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ajika","n":"Ajika","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Ajika is a flavored sauce or dip made mainly in Abkhazia and Mingrelia regions (Georgia). It is often used to flavor food. Ajika is primarily capsicum-based and usually includes other spices such as coriander or utskho suneli. Common varieties of ajika resemble Italian red pesto in appearance and consistency, although a dry version also exists. Though it is usually red, green ajika is also made with unripe peppers."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ajika"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajika"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Chashushuli","dish/chashushuli"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"]]}]},"dish/chicken-kiev":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chicken-kiev","n":"Chicken Kiev","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Russian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Chicken Kiev, also known as chicken Kyiv, is a dish made of chicken fillet pounded and rolled around cold butter, then coated with egg and bread crumbs, and either fried or baked. Since fillets are often referred to as suprêmes in professional cookery, the dish is also called \"suprême de volaille à la Kiev\". Stuffed chicken breast is generally known in Russian and Ukrainian cuisines as côtelette de volaille. Though it has disputed origins, the dish is particularly popular in the post-Soviet states, as well as in several other countries of the former Eastern Bloc, and in the English-speaking world."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chicken-kiev"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicken_Kiev"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Bread crumbs","ingredient/bread-crumbs"],["Breadcrumbs","ingredient/breadcrumbs"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Olga's Flavor Factory","ref":"site/olgasflavorfactory-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"olgasflavorfactory.com","note":"Russian-born home cook Olga's blog, with a dedicated Russian recipes category.","url":"https://www.olgasflavorfactory.com/category/russianrecipes/","suggested":true}]},