ezard","dish/sholezard"]]}]},"ingredient/silver-needle-noodles":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/silver-needle-noodles","n":"Silver needle noodles","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Silver needle noodle, rat noodle (老鼠粉; lǎo shǔ fěn), bee tai bak (simplified Chinese: 米筛目; traditional Chinese: 米篩目; Pe̍h-ōe-jī: bí-thai-ba̍k), runny nose vermicelli (Chinese: 鼻涕嘜; Jyutping: bei6 tai3 mak1)  or lot (Khmer: លត),  giam ee (เกี้ยมอี๋) is a variety of Chinese noodle. The noodles are short, about 5 cm long and 5 mm in diameter, and white semi-transparent in colour.  The noodles are available in many Chinese markets in Chinese populated areas such as Hong Kong, Taiwan, Cambodia, Indonesia, Malaysia, Brunei, Thailand, Vietnam and Singapore."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-silver-needle-noodles"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_needle_noodles"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Cambodian Chinese cuisine","cuisine/cambodian-chinese-cuisine",[["Lort cha","dish/lort-cha"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bean sprouts","ingredient/bean-sprouts"],["Chives","ingredient/chives"],["Dark soy sauce","ingredient/dark-soy-sauce"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Fried egg","ingredient/fried-egg"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Scallions","ingredient/scallions"]]}]},"dish/osechi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/osechi","n":"Osechi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Osechi-ryōri are traditional Japanese New Year foods. Osechi are easily recognizable by their special boxes called jūbako (重箱), which resemble bentō boxes. Like bentō boxes, jūbako are often kept stacked before and after use. Not all parts of Japan, such as Suzu in Ishikawa, practice the custom of eating osechi."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"k